  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Ethan Salas hit two home runs and the Padres never trailed, taking control early and coasting past Colorado at Coors Field.",
    "what_this_means": "Both sides clicking is the story here. Salas's two-run blast in the first inning gave San Diego an immediate lead they never surrendered, and he added two more runs with another homer in what became a 9-3 blowout. Robbie Ray held Colorado to three hits through six innings, striking out six, while David Morgan and Yuki Matsui combined for three scoreless frames in relief. The Padres scored in three separate innings. The first, fourth, and fifth. Turning the game into a rout by the fifth inning. This wasn't a scramble or a comeback. This was a team playing clean baseball early and letting the pitching hold.",
    "what_to_watch": "Salas's night matters because it shows what happens when the lineup connects and the starter gives you air to breathe. The Padres have leaned on pitching all season, but games like this. Where production spreads across multiple innings and a Game Driver finishes the work. Are how you stop treading water nine games back. Ray's line was clean. The bullpen arms stayed fresh. And the offense did enough at the right moments. This is exactly the kind of win that feels repeatable. Four runners in scoring position produced results; that's the margin they need to run. Ethan Salas just reminded them what they look like when both sides work.",
